WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:29.420
ai just turned a famously tricky poem into an interactive voice-narrated guide you can explore right now. This is Models &amp; Agents for Beginners, Episode 174. You know that feeling when a class reading looks like a wall of words and you cannot tell what any of it is pointing at? A researcher named Ethan Mollick asked an artificial intelligence system called Claude Fable 5.1 to open one of those walls. The poem is T.S. Eliot's The Waste Land.

00:00:29.420 --> 00:00:35.080
packed with mythology, history, and nods to other books. Most readers only ever

00:00:35.080 --> 00:00:39.440
scratch the surface. Instead of one straight reading, he got a website with

00:00:39.440 --> 00:00:44.000
several routes through the same text. It includes audio recordings, scholarly

00:00:44.000 --> 00:00:49.140
notes, and links that tie ideas together. Picture a really knowledgeable friend who

00:00:49.140 --> 00:00:53.340
can pull up a background fact, play a different voice, or jump to a related

00:00:53.340 --> 00:00:58.240
section the moment you get stuck. It is like a museum where every painting has a

00:00:58.240 --> 00:01:00.980
side door, and you choose which door to open.

00:01:01.680 --> 00:01:06.900
Molek shared the result on X, and the pathways, recordings, and scholarship links impressed

00:01:06.900 --> 00:01:07.220
him.

00:01:07.740 --> 00:01:13.100
For students, or anyone who loves books, music, or art, it behaves like a personal tutor

00:01:13.100 --> 00:01:15.060
that waits to see what you want next.

00:01:15.640 --> 00:01:17.660
You do not need to already be an expert.

00:01:18.200 --> 00:01:20.880
The guide simply takes the intimidation out of the room.

00:01:21.500 --> 00:01:25.720
Something that used to feel like homework starts to feel closer to a creative hobby.

00:01:25.720 --> 00:01:30.640
If you have ever wished a classic book or song came with a friendly guide that understood

00:01:30.640 --> 00:01:33.280
what confused you, this is a glimpse of that.

00:01:33.700 --> 00:01:39.020
The same researcher noted that AI remains a wonderful tool for exploring topics far from

00:01:39.020 --> 00:01:39.380
coding.

00:01:39.800 --> 00:01:42.280
You can try it right now on your phone or laptop.

00:01:42.800 --> 00:01:47.440
Search for the Wasteland Interactive Guide, open it, and click through the different pathways.

00:01:48.060 --> 00:01:52.760
Watch how each route offers a different way in, instead of forcing everyone down the

00:01:52.760 --> 00:01:53.340
same hallway.

00:01:53.340 --> 00:01:57.920
way. Here is the part that still makes my brain light up, once you peek under the hood.

00:01:58.600 --> 00:02:03.240
Your phone's keyboard already guesses the next word while you text, and sometimes it

00:02:03.240 --> 00:02:08.260
finishes the sentence before you do. Now stretch that same guessing game across a whole

00:02:08.260 --> 00:02:13.960
poem. Instead of one word, the system is guessing helpful paragraphs, voice lines, and links

00:02:13.960 --> 00:02:19.200
that connect ideas. First, it looks at the original lines, the way you glance at a tough

00:02:19.200 --> 00:02:25.100
paragraph in English class. Second, it draws on huge numbers of examples of how people explain

00:02:25.100 --> 00:02:31.000
difficult writing, like a teacher who has read a million study guides. Third, at each spot,

00:02:31.160 --> 00:02:35.780
it predicts what extra information would help most. That might be a quick definition,

00:02:36.100 --> 00:02:41.840
a historical note, or an audio clip that matches the mood. Fourth, because it can draft many

00:02:41.840 --> 00:02:47.460
versions at once, it can offer several routes through the same material. Nobody is forced down

00:02:47.460 --> 00:02:52.480
a single path, which is why it feels less like a textbook and more like a choose your own adventure

00:02:52.480 --> 00:02:58.460
written for you. Think of a tour guide who watches your face, then offers the next plaque, a short

00:02:58.460 --> 00:03:04.100
story, or a different room. The model is not memorizing the poem like a stack of flashcards.

00:03:04.700 --> 00:03:10.300
It is constantly predicting what a curious reader would need next. Once you see it as fancy auto

00:03:10.300 --> 00:03:16.560
complete with a library card, the magic gets a lot more human-sized. And that same start small,

00:03:16.560 --> 00:03:20.900
all, share everything's spirit, showed up in a totally different corner this week.

00:03:21.420 --> 00:03:27.340
Someone spent free time training a tiny language model from scratch on Arabic text and dialects,

00:03:27.560 --> 00:03:28.980
then shared the whole project.

00:03:29.620 --> 00:03:34.380
A language model is software that learns patterns and words so it can predict what should come

00:03:34.380 --> 00:03:34.780
next.

00:03:35.380 --> 00:03:41.460
This one is called Horus-Talik 0.2B base, and it has 0.2 billion parameters.

00:03:41.460 --> 00:03:46.720
parameters are the tiny knobs the model tunes as it learns like millions of

00:03:46.720 --> 00:03:52.280
little volume dials. The whole first training run cost just over $100 about

00:03:52.280 --> 00:03:57.440
the price of a video game. It ran for about 30 hours on one consumer graphics

00:03:57.440 --> 00:04:04.740
chip. That chip was a single NVIDIA RTX Pro 6,000 blackwell server edition GPU which

00:04:04.740 --> 00:04:10.360
stands for graphics processing unit. The kind of processor that also trains AI. It

00:04:10.360 --> 00:04:12.400
It learned from 6 billion training tokens.

00:04:13.080 --> 00:04:18.060
Tokens are little chunks of text, like puzzle pieces of language the model reads one after

00:04:18.060 --> 00:04:18.400
another.

00:04:19.060 --> 00:04:23.880
The creator stopped at pre-training, which is the first big reading stage, then released

00:04:23.880 --> 00:04:26.260
the model, the data sources, and the code.

00:04:26.880 --> 00:04:31.660
Everything went out under the MIT license, a permission style that lets others study

00:04:31.660 --> 00:04:33.300
and reuse the work for learning.

00:04:33.900 --> 00:04:39.580
If you are curious about languages or how models learn, go to the token AI site, search

00:04:39.580 --> 00:04:46.580
for Horus to leak 0.2B base and read the training details and data sources. Notice how small

00:04:46.580 --> 00:04:52.340
the budget is next to the usual giant lab story. Creators who think in shapes got a new helper

00:04:52.340 --> 00:04:58.020
too. High 3D just released an agent built specifically for making three-dimensional models.

00:04:58.560 --> 00:05:05.060
An agent here is software that can take steps and use tools, not just chat back. It pairs a language

00:05:05.060 --> 00:05:10.780
model with three-dimensional tools, so you describe what you want, then refine geometry and textures.

00:05:11.460 --> 00:05:15.920
You can even turn the result into something usable in games or three-dimensional printing.

00:05:16.640 --> 00:05:21.440
It is aimed at people who want to go from idea to finished object without learning every

00:05:21.440 --> 00:05:27.680
button in complex software. The agent includes reusable skills for optimization, animation,

00:05:28.020 --> 00:05:34.380
and turning high-poly models into playable demos. High-poly just means a model with tons of tiny

00:05:34.380 --> 00:05:40.120
surfaces like a statue with every wrinkle which is too heavy for a game until you simplify it.

00:05:40.880 --> 00:05:44.260
There is a Windows client and the workflow walks you through generating,

00:05:44.620 --> 00:05:50.040
revising and finishing a model. If you are into art, game design, or just making things,

00:05:50.480 --> 00:05:56.240
open the high 3D agent page and describe a simple object like a backpack or a tiny spaceship.

00:05:56.920 --> 00:06:00.980
Watch how it guides you through those steps instead of dumping you into a blank toolbox.

00:06:02.160 --> 00:06:07.240
Researchers copied a tiny trick from fruit flies to help AI learn new smells with almost

00:06:07.240 --> 00:06:07.940
no memory.

00:06:08.600 --> 00:06:15.340
A fruit fly brain contains only about 140,000 neurons, yet the new spy fly algorithm uses a

00:06:15.340 --> 00:06:18.360
spiking neural network modeled on that odor system.

00:06:19.040 --> 00:06:24.300
A spiking network fires in quick bursts, more like real brain cells than a steady calculator.

00:06:24.980 --> 00:06:29.600
The team at the Okinawa Institute of Science and Technology designed it for times when

00:06:29.600 --> 00:06:35.220
examples, memory, and computing power are limited. It did especially well with only a few

00:06:35.220 --> 00:06:40.800
examples of each odor, and it learned new categories without substantially forgetting earlier ones.

00:06:41.400 --> 00:06:45.800
Sometimes the smartest move is studying nature instead of just making models bigger.

00:06:46.640 --> 00:06:52.320
A voice AI system also just passed the strict federal security rules that normally block new tech

00:06:52.320 --> 00:06:58.400
from government phones and citizen services. The listing appears under the new FedRAMP 20 times

00:06:58.400 --> 00:07:03.960
automation framework as the first dedicated voice AI vendor authorized for agency use.

00:07:04.620 --> 00:07:09.640
FedRAMP is the US government's security checkout before agencies can put a tool on official

00:07:09.640 --> 00:07:15.740
systems. Public agencies may soon have conversational tools that feel more like talking to a helpful

00:07:15.740 --> 00:07:21.660
person than tapping through old phone menus. That's it for today. Remember, every AI

